<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Second Dominican Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1865 CE – 1916 CE · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The restored Dominican state of 1865 to 1916, marked by caudillo rule, Ulises Heureaux's dictatorship, a sugar boom and mounting debt to foreign creditors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The War of Restoration ended Spanish rule in 1865, re-establishing Dominican independence. The victorious generals and rival parties, the Reds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenaventura-baez">Buenaventura Báez</a> and the Blues led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregorio-luperon">Gregorio Luperón</a>, fought for power in a long series of revolts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenaventura-baez">Báez</a>'s six-year rule of 1868 to 1874 negotiated annexation to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, which President Grant supported but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">US Senate</a> rejected in 1870. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulises-heureaux">Ulises Heureaux</a> dominated from 1882, ruling as dictator from 1887 until his assassination in 1899. Cuban and American investors built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills in the southeast, and railways linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cibao">Cibao</a> to the coast. Heureaux's borrowing left huge foreign debts, and in 1905 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took control of Dominican customs to pay creditors.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-caceres">Ramón Cáceres</a> was assassinated in 1911, and renewed civil strife followed. In 1916 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">Marines</a> and installed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/military-government-of-santo-domingo">Military Government of Santo Domingo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-dominican-republic.jpg" alt="Second Dominican Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Dominican_First_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1865 CE – 1916 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Buenaventura Báez, Gregorio Luperón, Ulises Heureaux</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dominican Republic (1865-1916)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=second-dominican-republic" aria-label="Second Dominican Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second Dominican Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1749L3105 1749M3105 1749L3105 1749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3105 1749h0M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santo Domingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1890&amp;focus=second-dominican-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
